text:
sofia: FYI we just got an enterprise escalation — customer says their SSE stream frequently disconnects after 10-30s when their client is slow. They see partial messages and sometimes a socket write error (EPIPE / EAGAIN) on their side. They attached a few truncated event payloads.
jessica: Can you paste the exact error from their logs? also what proxy/front-door are they using (nginx, cloudflare, alb)?
sofia: customer says nginx + internal LB. Log excerpt: `write EPIPE at stream.write (internal_streams.js:...)` and our edge shows 502/504 intermittently. When it fails the model request in our logs completed normally server-side but connection closed during flush.
mikhail: Sounds like classic slow consumer/backpressure. Two common failure modes: 1) upstream buffer fills and proxy drops connection, 2) app doesn't handle res.write backpressure (Node/Go) and tries to write while socket is paused -> EPIPE.
daniel: Can we get the full server-side request id and sample headers? want to check Content-Encoding and Transfer-Encoding. If upstream used gzip or buffering then SSE will be chunked and may flush unpredictably.
sofia: request-id: rq_01G9X3Z8Y. Headers customer forwarded: `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, `Cache-Control: no-cache`, `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, `Authorization: Bearer <short-lived token>`.
eli: also check token lifetime — if token rotates mid-stream and our auth layer returns 401 we might terminate connection. I've seen clients interpret that as a write error too.
jessica: Customer repro steps requested. They provided a minimal repro: they call our /v1/stream endpoint, then pipe output through `pv -qL 200` to throttle read speed and see disconnect in ~20s.
daniel: Good repro. Two immediate action items: 1) server side: ensure code respects write() return value and waits on 'drain' before continuing to write. 2) proxy: disable buffering for SSE (nginx: `proxy_buffering off; proxy_max_temp_file_size 0; proxy_http_version 1.1; chunked_transfer_encoding on; proxy_set_header Connection '';`). Also ensure `proxy_read_timeout` is sufficiently high.
mikhail: Node example for backpressure handling: ````js
// pseudocode
const stream = getModelStream();
stream.on('data', chunk => {
  const ok = res.write(`data: ${chunk}\n\n`);
  if (!ok) {
    stream.pause();
    res.once('drain', () => stream.resume());
  }
});
````
If you ignore `ok` backpressure your process will accumulate memory and then socket writes return false/EPIPE.
sofia: customer also showed nginx configs where `proxy_buffering` was left default -> that will buffer output and then drop if slow client.
jessica: We should send a short checklist to customer and request a controlled repro session. Proposed checklist: 1) confirm proxy has buffering off; 2) set `proxy_http_version 1.1` and `Connection ''`; 3) confirm node app uses `res.flush()` / honors `res.write()` return value; 4) test with `curl --no-buffer` piped to `pv -qL 100` to reproduce; 5) confirm token TTL > expected stream time or implement short reconnect logic.
sofia: here's a concrete client repro we asked them to run: ````sh
# slow consumer repro (client side throttle)
curl -s -N "https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?model=whatever" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: text/event-stream" | pv -qL 100 > /dev/null
````
They said above triggers disconnect reliably.
daniel: Also share quick server-side repro harness they can run locally to compare. Minimal express example that intentionally pushes at fixed interval and handles drain: ````js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
  res.set({ 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream', 'Cache-Control': 'no-cache' });
  let i = 0;
  const iv = setInterval(() => {
    const ok = res.write(`data: msg ${i}\n\n`);
    i++;
    if (!ok) {
      console.log('backpressure, pausing writes');
      clearInterval(iv);
      res.once('drain', () => {
        console.log('drain, resuming');
        // resume interval
      });
    }
  }, 50);
  req.on('close', () => clearInterval(iv));
});
app.listen(3000);
````
If they run that behind nginx with buffering off and throttle client it should stay stable. If it drops only behind our platform we need to inspect edge/proxy logs.
mikhail: Operation notes for infra: check edge proxy logs for rq_01G9X3Z8Y — search for upstream sendfile/write errors, EAGAIN, or 502 mapping to read timeout. Also look at socket buffer fullness metrics and `tcp_retransmit` counts.
eli: I dug into our edge for rq_01G9X3Z8Y — found `upstream prematurely closed connection while reading response header from upstream` followed by `recv() failed (104: Connection reset by peer)` around the same time client reported EPIPE. That suggests our process closed connection when trying to write.
daniel: Why would we close? possibility: long GC pause or request handler error (unhandled promise rejection) when write returns false and code doesn't await drain and then throws. Also token refresh path: if auth middleware tries to refresh and returns error it may terminate stream.
jessica: Action plan for customer: 1) Quick config checks (send checklist). 2) If still repros, schedule a debug window and provide curl/pv repro. 3) We'll instrument a debug build of runtime for that request id to dump write() return values and drain events. 4) Recommend client implements reconnection/backoff for SSE and idempotent resume (if they can resume token/state).
sofia: also flagged billing/auth edge-case: if user's quota is exhausted we currently return a 429 which closes connection. Customer wants clearer semantics — if quota will be exhausted mid-stream can we send a terminal event before closing?
mikhail: Agree — usability improvement: send `event: error\ndata: {"type":"quota_exceeded"}\n\n` before closing so clients don't interpret as transport failure. Filing follow-up product ticket.
daniel: For now ops: instrument a non-prod route to reproduce fast. I'll add logs that record `res.write` return value and whether `drain` fired.
eli: I'll also increase edge socket timeouts for a while and monitor.
jessica: I'll reply to customer with the checklist, ask for their nginx config snippet, and propose a debug window. Also give them node code snippet to handle backpressure.
sofia: Customer responded: they turned off proxy buffering and increased token TTL; after that they still saw occasional partial outputs but much less frequent. They're available tomorrow for a debug session.
mikhail: Good. Keep trace rq_01G9X3Z8Y and any new IDs. If we see more with `Connection reset by peer` correlate with GC or worker restart events.
sofia: tagging deploy-bot to note scheduled debug: `deploy-bot: schedule-debug rq_01G9X3Z8Y customer=AcmeCorp window=2025-09-30T16:00Z`
deploy-bot: scheduled debug for rq_01G9X3Z8Y at 2025-09-30T16:00Z (UTC) — reminder 30m before.
jessica: OK closing this thread for now, will re-open with debug notes after tomorrow. Thanks team.
